<<<
coryphaeus. Spell -ae-; see Æ, Œ. Pl. -aez; see -us.
===
**coryphaeus.** Spell *-ae-*; see ^^Æ^^, ^^Œ^^. Pl. *-aeī*; see ^^-US^^.
>>>

<<<
cosaque. See FRENCH WORDS.
===
**cosaque.** See ^^FRENCH WORDS^^.
>>>

<<<
cose, COZe, COSy, cozy. The established spelling for the adjective (a Scotch word of doubtful origin) is cosy.
===
**cose, coze, cosy, cozy.** The established spelling for the adjective (a Scotch word of doubtful origin) is *cosy*.
>>>

<<<
The verb (& noun) meaning sit (sitting) by the fire &c. is a BACK- FORMATION from this, & therefore best spelt cose.
===
The verb (& noun) meaning *sit (sitting) by the fire* &c. is a ^^BACK-FORMATION^^ from this, & therefore best spelt *cose*.
>>>

<<<
The verb (& noun) meaning gossip or chat is referred to French causer talk, & the spelling coze, which is the usual one, is also desirable,
===
The verb (& noun) meaning *gossip* or *chat* is referred to French *causer* talk, & the spelling *coze*, which is the usual one, is also desirable,
>>>

<<<
The noun cosy or cozy meaning a kind of seat is probably from the adjective, though helped to become a noun by the French causeuse.
===
The noun *cosy* or *cozy* meaning a kind of seat is probably from the adjective, though helped to become a noun by the French *causeuse*.
>>>

<<<
The spellings recommended, then, are:—cosy, comfortable, seat, teapot-warmer; cose, sit at ease, spell of sitting so; coze, chat (n. & v.).
===
The spellings recommended, then, are:—*cosy*, comfortable, seat, teapot-warmer; *cose*, sit at ease, spell of sitting so; *coze*, chat (n. & v.).
>>>

<<<
cosset makes -cting, -eted; see -T-, -TT-.
===
**cosset** makes *-eting*, *-eted*; see ^^-T-^^, ^^-TT-^^.
>>>

<<<
coster(monger). See CURTAILED WORDS.
===
**coster(monger).** See ^^CURTAILED WORDS^^.
>>>

<<<
costume. The verb is késti’m. This pronunciation is probably commoner, & is preferred by the OED, for the noun also; but k6’sttum (see Noun & VERB ACCENT) is often heard,
===
**costume.** The verb is kŏstū'm. This pronunciation is probably commoner, & is preferred by the OED, for the noun also; but kŏ'stūm (see ^^NOUN^^ & ^^VERB ACCENT^^) is often heard.
>>>

<<<
cosy, -Zy. See COSE.
===
**cosy, -zy.** See ^^COSE^^.
>>>

<<<
cot(e). The word for bed is or was Anglo-Indian, is unconnected with the other words, & is always cot.
===
**cot(e).** The word for *bed* is or was Anglo-Indian, is unconnected with the other words, & is always *cot*.
>>>

<<<
The poetic word for cottage, & the word for shelter (usually seen in compounds, as sheep-c.), represent allied but separate old-English words;
===
The poetic word for *cottage*, & the word for *shelter* (usually seen in compounds, as *sheep-c.*), represent allied but separate old-English words;
>>>

<<<
cot is now invariable in the sense cottage, & cote usual in the sense shelter; the latter, however, whether spelt cote or cot, is usually pronounced k6t, especially in the commonest word dovecote.
===
*cot* is now invariable in the sense *cottage*, & *cote* usual in the sense *shelter*; the latter, however, whether spelt *cote* or *cot*, is usually pronounced kŏt, especially in the commonest word *dovecote*.
>>>

<<<
 cotemporary. See CONTEMPORARY.
===


**cotemporary.** See ^^CONTEMPORARY^^.
>>>

<<<
 co-tenant. See co-.
===


**co-tenant.** See ^^CO-^^.
>>>

<<<
 cothurnus. Pl. -ni; see -us. As a word for tragedy, c. is a BATTERED ORNAMENT, co-tidal.

See co-.

1351

E

cotili(ijon. Spell with the -i-.
===


**cothurnus.** Pl. *-nī*; see ^^-US^^. As a word for *tragedy*, c. is a ^^BATTERED ORNAMENT^^.

**co-tidal.** See ^^CO-^^.

**cotill(i)on.** Spell with the -i-.
>>>

<<<
cottar, cotter, cottier. The words are clearly distinguished from coftager by being applicable not to any one who lives in a cottage, but -to peasants doing so under certain conditions of tenure.
===
**cottar, cotter, cottier.** The words are clearly distinguished from *cottager* by being applicable not to any one who lives in a cottage, but to peasants doing so under certain conditions of tenure.
>>>

<<<
it is merely that the -tar, -ter, forms are more used of the Scotch variety, & -tier of the Trish.
===
it is merely that the *-tar, -ter,* forms are more used of the Scotch variety, & *-tier* of the Irish.
>>>

<<<
It would be well if cottar were made the sole form, cotter left to the pin or bolt, & cottier abandoned.
===
It would be well if *cottar* were made the sole form, *cotter* left to the pin or bolt, & *cottier* abandoned.
>>>

<<<
cotyledon. Pl. -ns; see -ON 3.
===
**cotyledon.** Pl. *-ns*; see ^^-ON^^ 3.
>>>

<<<
couch, bed, sofa, &c. As a general word for anything that is lain on, bed, lair, &c., the word is poetic only.
===
**couch, bed, sofa, &c.** As a general word for anything that is lain on, *bed, lair,* &c., the word is poetic only.
>>>

<<<
As a mere synonym for the ordinary word sofa in conversation, it is a GENTEELISM.
===
As a mere synonym for the ordinary word *sofa* in conversation, it is a ^^GENTEELISM^^.
>>>

<<<
couch, the weed. The OED prefers the pronunciation kowch, & describes kooch as that of the southern counties only.
===
**couch, the weed.** The OED prefers the pronunciation kowch, & describes ko͞och as that of the southern counties only.
>>>

<<<
couchant. Pronounce kow’chant.
===
**couchant.** Pronounce kow'ch*ant*.
>>>

<<<
could. For such forms as Could he see you now, see SUBJUNCTIVES.
===
**could.** For such forms as *Could he see you now*, see ^^SUBJUNCTIVES^^.
>>>

<<<
couleur de rose, coulisses, couloir. See FRENCH WORDS.
===
**couleur de rose, coulisses, couloir.** See ^^FRENCH WORDS^^.
>>>

<<<
coulomb. Pronounce koolé’m.
===
**coulomb.** Pronounce ko͞olŏ'm.
>>>

<<<
co(u)iter. Spell with -u-.
===
**co(u)lter.** Spell with -u-.
>>>

<<<
council, -sel, -cillor, -sellor. A board or assembly, & the meeting of such a body, has always -cil, & a member of it is -cillor.
===
**council, -sel, -cillor, -sellor.** A board or assembly, & the meeting of such a body, has always *-cil*, & a member of it is *-cillor*.
>>>

<<<
The abstract senses consultation, advice, secret (keep one’s c.), belong to -sel, & one who gives advice is, as such, a -sellor, though he may be a -cillor also;
===
The abstract senses *consultation, advice, secret* (*keep* one's c.), belong to *-sel*, & one who gives advice is, as such, a *-sellor*, though he may be a *-cillor* also;
>>>

<<<
my -cillors are the members of my (e.g., the king’s) council; my counsellors, those who advise me officially or otherwise.
===
*my -cillors* are the members of my (e.g., the king's) council; *my counsellors*, those who advise me officially or otherwise.
>>>

<<<
Counsel, however, has also the semi-concrete sense of the person or persons (never counsels) pleading for a party to a law-suit (The second of our three counsel was the best); the use is originally abstract, as when All the wealth & fashion stands for all the
===
*Counsel*, however, has also the semi-concrete sense of the person or persons (never *counsels*) pleading for a party to a law-suit (*The second of our three counsel was the best*); the use is originally abstract, as when *All the wealth & fashion* stands for *all the*
>>>
